Before you try anything I describe subsequently make sure you've
read about the "NAO" option that I propose at the end of this
posting!

PATH is a variable that instructs the "shell" where to look for
programs if you just type in their names from any place in the
filesystem hierarchy; usually that variable is defined/extended
in a file in your HOME directory called ".profile". If you have
such a file with a PATH definition just add a colon and the path
of the games directory to the existing PATH variable, e.g.

   PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/usr/X11R6/bin:/bin:/usr/games

or to modify a PATH variable predefined elsewhere in the system
add a line

   PATH=${PATH}:/usr/games

Or if you're already in the /usr/games directory where nethack is
installed just type

   ./nethack

and you don't need fiddling with PATH, but make sure nethack is
installed as executable. (The access via PATH will also require
nethack having the executable flag set.)

Another option (since /usr/local/nethack should just be a wrapper
shell script) is to invoke it as

   sh ./nethack

from the /usr/games directory.

